+/// the fragments multipart uploads have made durable but not yet published — every chunk a
+/// committed `part:` record names, and every chunk an in-flight owned `sidx:` entry stages —
+/// for each session listed under `mpu:`, whatever its state. Covering a session 0016 would
+/// not count (an `sidx:` entry of a session no longer `Open`) only keeps more.
+///
+/// Its three fields mirror the committed three: a placement the build can read and trust, a
+/// chunk it can name but not trust, and a record it cannot read at all. Each is a class of
+/// its own rather than folded into `placed`, `malformed` or `unresolvable`, because scrub and
+/// the drain-status surface read those three and must keep their answers
+/// ([`ReferenceSet::staged`]).
+#[derive(Default)]
+pub(crate) struct StagedSet {
+    /// `(dserver, fragment)` a staged record places: each pair of a committed part's chunk
+    /// ref, or of an owned entry's planned placement, exactly as a committed reference
+    /// contributes its own — so a copy of a staged fragment on a server its placement does
+    /// not name (a re-place's losing destination, carrying its own `orphan:` pre-mark) is
+    /// still judged on its evidence.
+    pub placed: HashSet<(DServerId, FragmentId)>,
+    /// Chunks **held whole**: a staged record names the chunk but cannot be trusted for it —
+    /// a placement that does not name exactly one D server per fragment, or an owned entry
+    /// under the chunk's own `sidx:` key whose value will not decode. Every fragment bearing
+    /// the id is protected, as a malformed committed placement's are (ADR-0045 decision 3:
+    /// fail safe, never reclaim on doubt), while every other fragment is still judged.
+    ///
+    /// Stricter than the committed rule on one point, deliberately: an **empty** placement is
+    /// held here, never identity-filled. The empty vector is a pre-M3 committed record's
+    /// spelling; a staged record is born full-length (`0016:828`), so here it can only be
+    /// damage.
+    ///
+    /// By chunk, then by the record's key exactly as the store spells it, valued by the fault
+    /// — attribution, so each consumer names the record to repair.
+    pub held: BTreeMap<ChunkId, BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, String>>,
+    /// Staged records the build could **not read at all** — a `part:` value that will not
+    /// decode, an `sidx:` key that names no chunk, an `mpu:` key that names no upload — by key,
+    /// valued by the fault. Each hides which chunks it protects, so while any is here the set
+    /// is incomplete for every pass that deletes or marks ([`ReferenceSet::is_incomplete`]).
+    pub unresolvable: BTreeMap<Vec<u8>, String>,
